MODULE WindowCustoms;

REQUIRE TestUtils;

// a navigator window keeps what draws it (EXTEND WINDOW ... CUSTOM) in two halves : a literal it starts with and a
// property computing it, with a placeholder for a literal a property comes without. Modules that do not require each
// other are parsed at once, each in a thread of its own, so two of them giving one window its halves wrote them at once :
// a lab start of such modules lost the literal to the placeholder, and the check that a React component is not given a
// property could be passed the same way. And which of two statements a half kept was the one written last, so the parse
// threads' timing : the halves are NF at the module's version now, so the later module's win, as when the modules were
// parsed one after the other. The NF writes take no lock of the window, the placeholder is read, not written, and the
// React component and the property are checked in the final window once every module has run

wcCustom () = '<div>wc</div>';

wcOrderFailure = DATA LOCAL STRING ();
wcSetInModuleOrder INTERNAL 'lsfusion.tests.SetWindowCustomsInModuleOrderAction' ();

testWindowCustomsInModuleOrder 'Of two modules giving a window its CUSTOM the later one in the module order wins' () {
    wcSetInModuleOrder();
    assertNull(wcOrderFailure(), wcOrderFailure());
}
